{"text":"The closest direct precedent is [BERT4Rec (Sun et al., 2019), §3.6](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.06690): alongside random bidirectional masked-item reconstruction, training includes examples masking only the final item to better match next-item inference. This supports combining supervision types within one shared model, but does not establish a particular fixed loss coefficient. Its one-mask ablation (§4.5) partly addresses masking multiplicity; it does not isolate terminal versus interior supervision with matched target counts, sampling frequency, and loss normalization.

[Disentangling Past-Future Modeling in Sequential Recommendation via Dual Networks (Zhang et al., CIKM 2022)](https://arxiv.org/html/2210.14577) explicitly diagnoses the mismatch between training with past-plus-future context and inference with past alone. Distinguish that diagnosis from DualRec’s architecture: separate past/future encoders, next-/previous-item objectives, and bidirectional knowledge transfer; only the past encoder serves inference. Its baseline comparisons and architectural ablations support the proposed framework, but do not constitute a controlled weighted terminal/interior-loss experiment within one BERT4Rec encoder, or directly establish that measured right-context dependence causes poorer next-item ranking.

I found no verified study satisfying all requested controls. [Turning Dross Into Gold Loss (2023)](https://arxiv.org/html/2309.07602) further cautions against architectural attribution: matching cross-entropy objectives substantially changes SASRec–BERT4Rec comparisons. Thus, the coefficient has qualitative precedent, while the context-mismatch explanation remains a hypothesis requiring controls. A cheap proposed diagnostic: using one existing checkpoint, compare identical masked targets with versus without right context, stratified by prefix length, and separately measure validation next-item ranking. This measures context reliance, not causality; subsequent coefficient ablations should normalize each loss separately and hold target frequency and compute constant."}
